<!-- METHODS -->
<!-- ===================================================== -->

<section id="methods">

<h2>8. Linear Equations Solve Karne ke Methods</h2>


<h3>Method 1 — Substitution Method</h3>

<div class="example">

Equations:

<br>

x + y = 10

<br>

x - y = 2

<br><br>

First equation se:

<br>

x = 10-y

<br><br>

Second mein substitute:

<br>

10-y-y=2

<br>

10-2y=2

<br>

2y=8

<br>

y=4

<br>

x=6

</div>


<h3>Method 2 — Elimination Method</h3>

<p>
Ek variable ko eliminate karne ke liye equations add/subtract karte hain.
</p>

<div class="example">

2x + y = 7

<br>

x - y = 2

<br><br>

Add:

<br>

3x = 9

<br>

x = 3

<br><br>

Then:

<br>

3-y=2

<br>

y=1

</div>


<h3>Method 3 — Matrix Inverse Method</h3>

<p>
Equations:
</p>

<div class="formula">

2x+y=7

<br>

x-y=2

</div>

<p>
Matrix:
</p>

<div class="matrix">

[ 2   1 ] [ x ]   [ 7 ]
[ 1  -1 ] [ y ] = [ 2 ]

</div>

<p>
AX=B
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<div class="formula">

X=A⁻¹B

</div>


<h3>Method 4 — Cramer's Rule</h3>

<p>
2 variables ke case mein:
</p>

<div class="formula">

x = Dₓ / D

<br><br>

y = Dᵧ / D

</div>

<p>
where D coefficient matrix ka determinant hai.
</p>


<h3>8.1 Cramer's Rule Example</h3>

<p>
Equations:
</p>

<div class="formula">

2x + y = 7

<br>

x - y = 2

</div>

<p>
Coefficient determinant:
</p>

<div class="matrix">

D =

| 2   1 |
| 1  -1 |

</div>

<div class="formula">

D = (2×-1)-(1×1)

<br>

= -2-1

<br>

= -3

</div>


<p>
Dx:
</p>

<div class="matrix">

Dx =

| 7   1 |
| 2  -1 |

</div>

<div class="formula">

Dx = -7 - 2 = -9

</div>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<div class="formula">

x = Dx/D

<br>

x = (-9)/(-3)

<br>

<b>x=3</b>

</div>


<p>
Dy:
</p>

<div class="matrix">

Dy =

| 2  7 |
| 1  2 |

</div>

<div class="formula">

Dy = 4-7

<br>

= -3

</div>

<div class="formula">

y = Dy/D

<br>

= (-3)/(-3)

<br>

<b>y=1</b>

</div>


<h3>8.2 Three Equations ka General Form</h3>

<p>
Teen variables:
</p>

<div class="formula">

a₁x+b₁y+c₁z=d₁

<br>

a₂x+b₂y+c₂z=d₂

<br>

a₃x+b₃y+c₃z=d₃

</div>

<p>
Matrix form:
</p>

<div class="matrix">

[ a₁ b₁ c₁ ] [ x ]   [ d₁ ]
[ a₂ b₂ c₂ ] [ y ] = [ d₂ ]
[ a₃ b₃ c₃ ] [ z ]   [ d₃ ]

</div>


<h3>8.3 Possible Solutions</h3>

<p>
Linear equations ke generally 3 situations ho sakte hain:
</p>

<table>

<tr>
<th>Situation</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Unique Solution</td>
<td>Exactly one solution</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>No Solution</td>
<td>Equations inconsistent hain</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Infinitely Many Solutions</td>
<td>Bahut saare solutions possible hain</td>
</tr>

</table>


<h3>8.4 Unique Solution</h3>

<div class="example">

x+y=10

<br>

x-y=2

<br><br>

Solution:

x=6, y=4

</div>


<h3>8.5 No Solution</h3>

<div class="example">

x+y=5

<br>

x+y=10

</div>

<p>
Same left side but different right side.
Dono simultaneously true nahi ho sakti.
</p>


<h3>8.6 Infinite Solutions</h3>

<div class="example">

x+y=5

<br>

2x+2y=10

</div>

<p>
Second equation first equation ka multiple hai.
Isliye infinitely many solutions possible hain.
</p>

</section>


